onnected, leaving pieces interspersed with the opponent\'s.\n3.  **(D, 6)**: This move flips D5 and places a piece at D6. This creates a solid vertical line of friendly pieces (D4, D5, D6). It limits the opponent\'s immediate responses to the C-column (C4 and C6). Creating a "wall" like this is generally stable in the opening and forces the opponent to play to the outside.\n\nReason: Move (D, 6) is the superior choice because it minimizes the opponent\'s mobility effectively while building a compact, connected group of pieces. In contrast, (F, 4) gives the opponent too many options, and (F, 6) leads to a less stable, scattered configuration.\n\nAnswer: (D, 6)'}
