g mobility. **So, I\'m choosing (A, 5) because it minimizes risks and maximizes potential for future moves!**\n\n\n', 'output': 'Situation: The game is in the early-to-mid stage. I am playing as \'@\' (Black) with 5 pieces, while the opponent \'*\' (White) has a dominant central mass of 18 pieces. The opponent controls the center and the lower-left quadrant. My goal is to maintain mobility by making quiet moves that do not flip too many pieces, forcing the opponent to break their stable structure.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(A, 5)**: This is a classic quiet move on the edge. It flips only one piece (A4). It establishes a stable presence on the A-row without exposing me to corner attacks (it is not a C-square) or opening up the board significantly. This preserves my mobility.\n- **(F, 6)**: This move extends into the empty southeast area and flips two pieces (E5, E6). While a reasonable development move, it is "louder" than A5 and gives the opponent more surface area to attack.\n- **(G, 3)**: This move is strategically poor for this stage. It flips a long line of pieces (Column 3 and diagonal), turning many of the opponent\'s pieces into mine. This drastically reduces my future move options while giving the opponent many new squares to play on.\n- **(B, 2), (G, 1)**: These are C-square moves (squares diagonally adjacent to corners). Playing these usually offers the opponent a tactical advantage to capture the adjacent corners (A1 or H1).\n- **(D, 1), (E, 1)**: These are edge moves, but they flip internal pieces and are less stable than A5.\n\nReason: I choose **(A, 5)** because it maximizes my chances of winning by adhering to the strategy of mobility and minimization. By flipping only one piece, I limit the opponent\'s possible responses and keep the board "closed," putting pressure on them to make a suboptimal move that might give me access to corners or stable edges later.\n\nAnswer: (A, 5)'}
