n the periphery. The bottom two rows (G and H) are completely empty. My strategy is to limit the opponent\'s mobility by playing safe, central moves ("quiet moves") and forcing them to play into the outer edges or the empty bottom rows, which will give me targets to attack later.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **(C, 6)**: This is a strong central move. It fills a gap in the existing formation and flips pieces in multiple directions (C4, C5, B5, D6, E6) without extending my presence into the vulnerable outer edges or the empty G/H rows. It keeps my group tight and reduces the opponent\'s available inner moves.\n- **(D, 8)**: This is an edge move. While taking an edge can be good, this move flips C7 and B6. Flipping C7 to my color likely gives the opponent a valid move at C8 (anchoring off their piece at C1). Giving the opponent easy edge access is risky.\n- **(G, 1-6)**: These moves break into the empty "frontier" at the bottom of the board. Playing here usually gives the opponent easy responses (waiting moves) and should be avoided to preserve tempo.\n- **(B, 1), (F, 1), (B, 7), (F, 7)**: These are moves onto C-squares or near X-squares. They are structurally weak squares that often lead to losing corners (A1, A8, H1, H8) and should be avoided if better moves exist.\n\nReason: Move (C, 6) is the most strategically sound. It maximizes my stability by keeping the game centered and minimizes the opponent\'s opportunities to counter-attack on the edges. It avoids the pitfalls of opening the empty bottom rows or playing weak squares adjacent to corners.\n\nAnswer: (C, 6)'}
