ieces on the right. My goal is to consolidate my territory on the edges and restrict the opponent\'s mobility without opening up corners for them.\n\nEvaluation: \nTwo edge moves stand out: (A, 6) and (F, 1).\n- (F, 1) extends the strong left-column wall from A1-E1 to F1. It flips two internal pieces (E2, D3) and is very stable.\n- (A, 6) fills the gap on the top edge, extending the wall from A1-A5 to A6. It flips only one piece (B6).\nComparing the two, (A, 6) is slightly preferable because it is a "quieter" move (flips fewer pieces), which is generally better to limit the opponent\'s options. More importantly, placing a piece at A6 and flipping B6 creates a configuration that prevents the opponent from easily accessing the top-right corner (A8). If the opponent later plays to B7 (a C-square) to fight back, it may give me a diagonal anchor (via C6) to take the corner at A8. Furthermore, A6 blocks horizontal access to A8 for the opponent.\n\nReason: Move (A, 6) secures the top edge, limits the opponent\'s mobility by flipping only one piece, and potentially sets up a trap for the top-right corner (A8) by encouraging or forcing the opponent into bad positions like B7. It is a stable, low-risk move that strengthens my control of the board.\n\nAnswer: (A, 6)'}
